The code statements that lead to the bug are:
u8 last_byte = *(data + length - 1);
Reason:
The `length` parameter is used directly as an offset into `data` without any validation. When `length` is zero or greater than the actual RX buffer size, this read accesses memory beyond the allocated region, triggering a slab-out-of-bounds error.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (unlikely(!length))
	return false;
if (unlikely(length > adapter->rx_buffer_len))
	return false;
last_byte = *(data + length - 1);
Reason:
The two guard clauses reject frames with invalid length before any memory access. The first check handles zero-length frames; the second ensures that `length` does not exceed the allocated RX buffer size (`adapter->rx_buffer_len`). Only after these validations does the code read the last byte, preventing the out‑of‑bounds access.

